datin_imkt_data:false_brinelling_susceptibility_under_varying_contact_conditions a prov:Entity, foaf:Project;
    dcterms:description "Oscillating, grease-lubricated rolling contacts can suffer from a wear mechanism commonly referred to as false brinelling.\nWhen dealing with this wear mechanism in research and practice, the grease formulation is usually the focus. To evaluate\ngrease performance under oscillation, standardised tests are available that use a fixed bearing type and load. This keeps\ntest results for different greases comparable, but makes it difficult to transfer the results to a given application in which\nthe aforementioned parameters likely differ. Therefore, this study uses an experimental method that employs a speed\ndependent critical threshold for false brinelling initiation to investigate the influence of load, contact kinematics, and\ngrease volume. The article discusses the physical influence of these parameters on the contact’s susceptibility to false\nbrinelling. Based on a physical perception of base oil demand and base oil availability to the contact, the experimental\nresults are used to propose a method that allows the prediction of the critical threshold for the initiation of false brinelling\nfor a given application. These findings represent a step towards a more holistic, application-related approach to false\nbrinelling. A possible application is pitch bearings in wind turbines, where contact types, pressure, and size differ largely\nfrom the conditions of standardised tests.";
    dcterms:title "False Brinelling Susceptibility under Varying Contact Conditions";
    rdfs:label "False Brinelling Susceptibility under Varying Contact Conditions";
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-11T16:55:20.354+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-11T16:55:20.354+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime.